WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.430
- It doesn't matter.

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:03.360
Nothing matters.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.250
- [Diane] Wow, what happened in Malibu?

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.083
- I got to go.

00:00:06.083 --> 00:00:07.660
- So, I think a big turning point

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.000
for our show happens in that first season,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.150
at, really, episode
seven and episode eight,

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.290
which are called Say
Anything, and The Telescope.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.670
Funnily enough, we only
sent the first six episodes

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:20.540
to critics, which looking back,
might have been a mistake,

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.020
because I think they were
some of the first audience

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.300
who didn't really get
what we were going for,

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.650
because, maybe we didn't tell them.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.000
Maybe, we kept it a little
too close to the vest

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.410
that right around the corner,

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:31.650
there were these two big episodes

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.300
that kind of changed the whole thing.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.950
And so, Say Anything is episode seven,

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:37.280
and that's an episode that's entirely told

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.090
from Princess Carolyn's perspective,

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.050
up to that moment,
everything, almost everything

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.400
that's happened in the show
has been through Bojack.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.320
A few episodes had small
B-stories or C-stories,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.615
but they're really heavy A-story episodes.

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:49.540
And then, you kind of switch it,

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.737
and be like, "No, Bojack is
the side story this time.

00:00:52.737 --> 00:00:54.780
"It's really focused on
this other character."

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.630
It felt like a bit of a departure for us,

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.790
and we wanted to kind of explore

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:00.900
what is the damage that Bojack causes

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.560
because when he is your central character,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.900
you're with him, you know,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.900
he kind of falls into
some sort of wreckage

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.990
and kind of, steps out and
like, dusts himself off,

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:09.990
and wanders into the next misadventure,

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.100
but we wanted it through Princess Carolyn,

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.580
explore what is it like
being another person

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:15.510
in this person's life.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.590
What is it like attaching
yourself to this kind of toxicity?

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:20.510
And that episode, Say Anything,

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.910
has the first really sad
ending of the series,

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.570
that everything up until
then had been pretty light,

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.500
or maybe we'd end on
like a dark kind of joke,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.100
but Say Anything really
ends on like, a serious kind

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.370
of sad moment with this lonely character.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.650
And that for me was a real sign

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.010
of where we wanted to go.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.760
And the next episode, The Telescope,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.310
I think, kind of, pushes
that into overdrive.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:43.770
That's the episode where Bojack goes

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.080
to visit his old friend, Herb,

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.000
and it's kind of set up
as this special episode

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.890
where Bojack's gonna make
amends with this guy,

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.730
and he doesn't, and Herb
refuses to forgive him

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.090
for this thing that
happened in their past,

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.250
and kind of, because of our expectations

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.370
from watching television,

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.600
and because of Bojack's own expectations,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.330
you know, learning most
of what he's learned

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:03.400
through making television,

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.590
he really expects, at the
end, this is gonna work out,

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.070
and it really doesn't,

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.000
and that also is a sign to our audience

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.610
that we're telling our stories
a little differently here,

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.855
and we're doing things
a little differently.

00:02:14.855 --> 00:02:17.522
(feet thumping)

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.430
I like to lean into the gimmick.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.600
I like to say, "Okay, well, let's do it."

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.210
And then, what does that mean?

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.820
And what does this story

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.440
that would necessitate
that kind of storytelling?

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.190
Because I think the form should
always match the function.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:34.930
I think you want to have
a format for a story

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.360
that goes hand-in-hand with
this story you're telling,

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.260
but it doesn't necessarily mean,

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.830
you need to have the story first,

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.140
and then, come up with the format.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:43.690
A lot of times, you can
start with the format.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.127
You can start with, "Let's tell an episode

00:02:45.127 --> 00:02:46.310
"without any dialogue."

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.870
And so, that episode,
specifically, Fish Out of Water,

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:49.940
you know, I came into the writer's room,

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.087
and I said, "I want to do a story

00:02:52.087 --> 00:02:53.500
"where none of the characters talked."

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.010
And that was a challenge for the meeting

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.800
'cause like, how do you justify that?

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:57.770
That was the first challenge

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.377
because you didn't want to just be like,

00:02:59.377 --> 00:03:01.417
"Oh, we're in Charlie Chaplin land,

00:03:01.417 --> 00:03:03.230
"you know, for no reason."

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.520
And that, characters are
just kind of like looking

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.670
at each other and Mister Bean-ing it up

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.330
when it's these characters
that we've known

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:13.590
for three seasons to have
full auditory functionality.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.490
So, you need something
to kind of, explains

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:18.020
why the characters wouldn't be talking.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.250
Kind of separately to that,

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.590
Mike Hollingsworth is
our supervising director,

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.350
and he'd been campaigning
me to do an underwater story

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.800
because at the end of season two,

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.800
we had an episode with a boat,

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.270
which is like a throwaway scene.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:32.190
There's a boat on the dock,

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.540
and you see like, a gangplank of people

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.530
from the dock walking into the boat.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.500
And there's another gangplank
going off into the ocean,

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.660
and you see all these fish
walking up the gangplank

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:41.493
into the boat.

00:03:41.493 --> 00:03:43.720
So that like, eluded
to something going on,

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:44.850
underneath the water,

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.060
and so, Mike was really fastened

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.427
and I'm like, "What's
happening in underwater world?

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:50.470
"Let's tell a story about that."

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:54.360
And then, also, provided
some complexity to me

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:55.370
because I didn't understand

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.100
how you could tell a story
underwater with our characters,

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.120
who were all air-breathing,
land-dwelling mammals, right?

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.340
How do you tell a story underwater

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.020
with our characters who
don't live underwater?

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.210
And then, how would they even
communicate to each other?

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.480
I remember one night, waking
up in the middle of the night,

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.207
and I realized, "Eureka!

00:04:13.207 --> 00:04:15.090
"These questions answer each other."

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.970
On the one hand, how do
you tell a silent story?

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:18.300
How do you justify it?

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.040
How do you tell an underwater story?

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.680
Put the peanut butter in the chocolate,

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.230
and together it makes Fish Out of Water.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.380
So then, the room tried to
figure out how to do it,

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:27.930
and that episode was written

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.440
by Jordan Young and Elijah
Aaron, two brilliant writers,

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.637
and I said, "How do you
write an episode like that?"

00:04:33.637 --> 00:04:35.600
And they said, "We don't
know, let's figure it out."

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.380
So, we talked through the story,

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:39.700
and we, you know, kind of
put it up on the whiteboard

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:41.150
like you do any other episode,

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:42.470
and then, when it was time to write it,

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:43.440
they wrote the whole episode,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.040
just without the dialogue,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.820
just, you know, stage directions.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.060
Bojack, you know, walks
through the rooms of the hotel.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:50.900
He looks out.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.910
He sees a bunch of sardines
cram themselves into a room,

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:56.540
you know, all these joke,
but just written out,

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.780
but they didn't know how
long to make the script

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.100
because you have certain benchmarks.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.170
You have an understanding

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:04.090
of like, how many words per minute?

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:06.510
You know, three seasons
in, you kind of get a sense

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:08.280
of how long a script should be,

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.680
but we didn't know the timing
for how long the script is.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:12.740
They just wrote a script.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:15.010
We sent it to Mike Hollingsworth,
our supervising director,

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.730
who end up directing that episode.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:20.670
And then, he timed it out by acting it.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.580
He read through it and
kind of, did the motions,

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.830
and timed himself, thinking
how long is this gonna take.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:28.520
And so, we ended up getting it to time,

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.160
based on Mike's estimates.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:32.831
- Hello, you're always,

00:05:32.831 --> 00:05:34.981
Why don't you go outside
and play or something?

00:05:34.981 --> 00:05:37.185
- Bye, mom.
- You are fired.

00:05:37.185 --> 00:05:38.340
Goodbye, thank you.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.560
- Time's Arrow, in season four,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.990
is also a big benchmark episode.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:46.930
I think, part of it was
structurally looking at the season,

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:48.470
and up until that point,

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:51.080
every episode eleven had been kind

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:54.140
of a big bravura episode for us.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:56.090
I mean, the first season,
it was a crazy drug trip,

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:58.730
and the second season, it was
this sojourn into New Mexico,

00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:02.140
and the third season, it
was a very tragic drug binge

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:04.700
that had really been the
climax for the season.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.540
And in all of those episodes,

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.440
they're mostly about
Bojack behaving terribly

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:10.860
and ruining things,

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.210
and it felt like four seasons in,

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:13.880
we can't go to that well again.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.550
We have to think of like
a new way to tell a story

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:19.220
that still feels like a
climax for the season,

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:21.250
but isn't just about
Bojack being a schmuck.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:22.147
And so, part of the idea was,

00:06:22.147 --> 00:06:23.437
"Well, can we reverse that a little bit?

00:06:23.437 --> 00:06:25.960
"Can we give Bojack a
bit of a hero moment?"

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.300
Which the end of the episode
does actually provide,

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:32.080
and is a very cathartic
climax for the season.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.170
In kind of an opposite way

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.110
of how most of our seasons operate.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:38.530
And then, we were talking
about this character

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.710
of Beatrice, Bojack's mother,

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:43.510
who the whole season is
suffering from dementia,

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:44.857
and we thought, "Wouldn't it be cool

00:06:44.857 --> 00:06:46.870
"to tell a story from her point of view?"

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.317
And see the world as she sees it

00:06:49.317 --> 00:06:51.010
and is experiencing it,

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:53.020
and that a lot of the
kind of non-sequiturs

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:55.750
that, maybe, she's been motioning
to throughout the season,

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.300
we get further context for 'em.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.600
We'd see her story and her life.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.910
And I think a big theme of the show

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:04.060
is that nobody is just
a hero or a villain,

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.280
that all of us are complex,
flawed individuals.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.530
And so, to paint a picture of Beatrice,

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:12.710
who up until this point,
had been pretty terrifying,

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:17.090
and awful, and painted as
solely a burden to Bojack

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:18.200
in the present,

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.590
and a scarring influence
on him in the past,

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.510
but they try to draw her sympathetically,

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:24.960
and make people care about her.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.970
That felt like a really
fun, interesting challenge.

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:29.940
So, that's kind of the
beginning of that episode.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.530
I think, meanwhile, that episode
was written by Kate Purdy,

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.300
who had written on our
show since season one,

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:36.530
is a brilliant writer,

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:38.877
and at that point, Kate and I
were developing this new show,

00:07:38.877 --> 00:07:42.050
"Undone," for Amazon, which
plays a lot with time,

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:43.830
and people falling through time,

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.960
and unsure of where they
are and what they are.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.350
So, in some ways,

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:49.280
Time's Arrow was kind of
a test run for "Undone."

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:50.727
It was like, "Let's play around with this.

00:07:50.727 --> 00:07:52.157
"Like, will this be too difficult

00:07:52.157 --> 00:07:53.537
"for our audience to follow?

00:07:53.537 --> 00:07:56.017
"Can we jump around in time in this way,

00:07:56.017 --> 00:07:57.657
"you know, through this perspective

00:07:57.657 --> 00:08:00.240
"without really explaining
it as it's going?"

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.210
And it was very encouraging.

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:02.450
I think the test was successful,

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:04.640
and made us want to explore
more with our other show.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.140
- The only thing that
matters is right now,

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:10.100
this moment, this one spectacular moment

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.043
we are sharing together, right Sarah Lynn?

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.133
Sarah Lynn?

00:08:16.133 --> 00:08:19.530
- One of the tenets of the show, to me,

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.940
was that it was not a
typical animated show

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:23.710
that snaps back to status quo at the end

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:25.000
of every episode.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.920
I remember having a meeting very early on

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:31.070
with some of the designers,
and the directors,

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.000
and talking about the look of the show,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.630
and the feel of it,

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.780
and I remember saying that, you know,

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.773
if Bojack has like a big
party in episode three,

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.280
and all the furniture gets screwed up,

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:46.460
in episode four, the furniture
should still be screwed up.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:48.100
That, you know, kind
of, we're used to shows

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:49.110
that just kind of, it ends,

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:51.530
and the next episode,
everything's back to normal,

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:54.280
and I want that damage to linger.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.370
And that kind of became a
metaphor for the show itself,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:59.700
and the emotional toll that
the events of the show take

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:01.910
on the characters, and then,
I want you to, kind of, feel

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:03.090
over the course of that first season,

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:04.220
and then, in the subsequent seasons,

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:06.610
that the damage that has
happened to this character

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:08.920
and to his relationships have had weight,

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:11.200
and have had effect,

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.170
and that if someone gets in
a big fight in one episode,

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.380
it's not gonna go back to
normal the next episode.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.200
He's still thinking about all the stuff

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.033
that's happened to him,

00:09:19.033 --> 00:09:20.670
and the things that he's done,

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:22.090
even if he's not mentioning it, right?

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:24.127
He's not necessarily saying,

00:09:24.127 --> 00:09:25.167
"Oh, I still feel guilty

00:09:25.167 --> 00:09:27.310
"over that thing I did four episodes ago,"

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:28.820
but we know it's still in there,

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:30.080
and occasionally, there are reminders,

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:31.720
where things will bubble back up

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:35.050
or think about all the history
that's happened to him.

00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:37.920
And so, I think a big part of that idea

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:39.970
or what goes hand-in-hand with that idea

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:43.250
of that the damage lingers
is that people will die,

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:45.480
and that when people die, they are dead,

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.550
and they're not coming back.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:49.460
And so, it felt important
to tell that story.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:51.440
We've actually told it a
few times on this show now,

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:52.833
but we wanted,

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:56.390
we wanted to do it.

00:09:56.390 --> 00:10:01.080
(laughs) We wanted to play out
the effects of these actions

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:02.890
as we kind of put it into place,

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:05.200
especially when you talk
about the end of season three,

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.300
it felt like we've been
setting up these dominoes

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:09.410
for awhile, like really, since, you know,

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:11.650
I'd say the third episode of season one.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:14.830
And so, by this point, you
know, we got to knock them over

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:15.970
or what are we doing?

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:17.730
- Are we judging you as the best booty?

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:19.580
- Whoa, Bojack, no.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:20.750
That is so degrading.

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:24.303
We've had a lot of incredible
guest stars over the years,

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:28.930
and usually, it just happens
because we write a script,

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:31.570
and we think who would be a
good person for this script?

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:33.890
Like, who would be the funniest person

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:38.890
to be judging this booty
competition reality show?

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:40.720
What if it's Felicity Huffman?

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.010
And then, we go to them,

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:45.640
and they usually say yes, which is crazy.

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.327
Occasionally, we'll hear something like,

00:10:47.327 --> 00:10:49.380
"Oh, this person's a big fan of the show."

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:51.570
And then, we'll keep them in mind

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:52.810
if there's a part for them to play.

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:55.320
We don't usually write
parts for the actors.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:56.860
The scripts always come first,

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:57.857
and then, we think about,

00:10:57.857 --> 00:10:59.160
"Well, who should we get to do this?"

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:00.760
And again, our casting director,

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:01.960
Linda Lamontagne is incredible,

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:03.570
and she just kind of
has an ear to the ground

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.270
of who's looking to do animation,

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:07.940
who's looking to do
our show, specifically,

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.460
who would be fun and surprising,

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:11.370
and so, we get these lists from her,

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:13.210
and it's like, "Oh my God,
you think they'd say yes?"

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:14.450
Like, "We'll ask them."

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:16.537
And sometimes we ask
them, and they say, "No."

00:11:16.537 --> 00:11:17.580
but then, they say, "Yes."

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:18.910
and it's very exciting.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:21.360
But then, we've had
people come on the show

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:23.550
or people that I've seen
who have been heroes

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.070
of mine tell me they're fans of the show.

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:27.470
That completely blows my mind.

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:29.110
Like to have Weird Al Yankovic,

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:32.040
who I like grew up with, come
be a guest star on the show,

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:34.960
and tell me personally that
he really loves the show.

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:37.090
My brain can't process that.

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:40.450
I don't know how to take
that kind of compliment,

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:42.900
and I quickly change the
subject to something else.

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.470
- Herb, I said I'm sorry.

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:46.840
- Yeah, and I do not forgive you.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:50.550
- You know, I think it is
up to the individual viewer

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:54.060
to decide for themselves
if Bojack is forgivable,

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:57.600
or redeemable, or deserving of that.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:02.600
I personally like to believe
that we are all flawed people,

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:10.130
and we are all deserving of forgiveness,

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:12.970
and redemption, and grace,
but we have to work for it.

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:14.020
We're not owed it.

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:14.853
You know what I mean?

00:12:14.853 --> 00:12:15.920
We are worthy of it,

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:18.160
but maybe, deserving is the wrong word,

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:19.830
but it's open to interpretation,

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:20.890
and I grapple with that.

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:22.040
I don't always know myself,

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:24.700
and I think, one of the
joys of making the show

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:26.210
is presenting these questions

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:28.070
and not always having the answers,

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:30.810
and accenting sometimes, when
you don't have the answer,

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:32.100
but you feel like you need an answer,

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:33.740
and you put it in your work,

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:35.950
it feels fake or it feel forced.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:38.060
And so, a lot of times, you
know, we'll have questions,

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:39.410
or we'll talk about it
in the writer's room,

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:40.690
we'll go back and forth,

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:41.760
and at the end of the day,

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:43.910
if it feels like we don't
have a hard answer to this,

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.210
we'll just kind of leave the
audience with the question,

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:47.640
and it's up to them.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:49.390
- [Bojack] I look back
at that other Bojack

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:51.137
and I think, "Who is that?"

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:54.750
- My favorite reaction to the show

00:12:56.210 --> 00:12:59.070
that has happened quite a few times now,

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:01.060
and at first I didn't quite
know what to do with it,

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:03.310
and now when it happens,
I say, "Thank you,"

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:05.270
is when people tell me

00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:08.110
that the show has helped them articulate

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:11.340
or identify something about themselves

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.630
that they've never been able to recognize

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:15.390
or verbalize before,

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:18.080
that the show has helped
them talk to a therapist,

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:23.080
talk to a loved one, realize a truth

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:24.100
that has always been there,

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:26.110
but they've never quite
had the words for it.

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:27.180
Now, they have something to point to,

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:29.810
and go, "Oh, it's like that."

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:32.720
That was not necessarily
what we set out to do

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:34.170
when we were making the show,

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.580
but that feels so much
more rewarding to me,

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.497
than when people say,
"It's the funny animals.

00:13:38.497 --> 00:13:39.660
"It makes me laugh."

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:41.960
I'm glad the funny animals
make people laugh too,

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:43.950
like that definitely is
something we aim for,

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:45.950
and we're very proud of that, as well,

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:49.410
but I would hope that the
lasting legacy of the show

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:51.770
is that people continue to discover it,

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:54.650
and it continues to
articulate things for them

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:56.640
about feelings they've felt,

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:58.730
or ways in which they've seen the world,

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:01.810
and it allows them to, maybe, get help

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:04.293
that they didn't quite
know how to ask for before.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:05.983
- Nailed it.

